 <iati-activity default-currency="CHF" hierarchy="2" humanitarian="0" iati-activities:linked-data-default="http://iati.icc.example.org/" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-30011-P04620-AVMZ-00019</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-30011" type="15">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Union for Conservation of Nature</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mainstreaming an Integrated Water Resources Manage</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IUCN in collaboration with the Government of Mozambique Water Resources Management Directorate (DNGARH) will implement an Integrated Water Management Resources Programme, MozWater: 2024-2027, for Mozambique. The MozWater Project will work in Mozambique to achieve water security through effective integrated water resources management in key basins and at a national level. It is a collaborative effort where joint action on the ground will produce key lessons and best practices that inform policy to strengthen business approaches, institutions, and rules around the sustainable use of land and water. MozWater will be implemented collaboratively with Administra&#xe7;&#xe3;o Regional &#xc1;guas do Norte (ARA Norte), Administra&#xe7;&#xe3;o Regional de &#xc1;guas do Centro (ARA Centro) and the National Directorate for Water Resources Management (DNGRH) as primary implementing partners. While other partners may be invited to deliver specific outputs, based on their thematic expertise and local impact and following the project design phase. It is envisaged that the National Disaster Management Institute (INGD) and National Meteorology Institute (INAM) will play important roles.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The focus for the project is to continue and scale up the previous programme, focusing on improved collaboration and stakeholders and investors synergies, access to safe water and to water for economic activities. The aggregated output to this programme will be increased climate resilience and improved sustainable livelihoods, including the promotion of alternative income generating activities and knowledge to plan and adapt in accordance with the landscapes&#xb4; evolution. Along project development, and with Government counterparts and IUCN members and partners, the collaborative sub-national stakeholders and implementing partners</narrative>
  </description>
